WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
Welcome to unintended consequences, episode 67.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.340
This is a show about what happens

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:08.220
when smart, well-meaning people try to fix something

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.020
and the world fixes back.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.640
Marketed as an indestructible shield against fire,

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:16.540
asbestos fibers remained in lungs for decades

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.060
and triggered a slow epidemic of cancer

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.340
that regulators could not see in time.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:25.320
In 1943, welders at the Brooklyn Navy Yard

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.640
sprayed a gray, fibrous mixture onto the bulkheads

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.100
of destroyer escorts.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.680
They applied the coating specifically

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.220
so those ships could survive torpedo hits

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.500
and engine room fires that might otherwise

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:39.740
spread through metal compartments in minutes.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.920
The material itself was light enough

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.140
not to add dangerous weight to the hull,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.500
inexpensive to produce at scale

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.580
and advertised as a permanent fix

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.240
that would never need replacement.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.820
30 years later, many of those same workers sat

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.140
in hospital rooms while doctors explained

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:59.840
that the fibers they had breathed in during the war

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.300
had never left their lungs.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.200
Those fibers were now producing mesothelomia tumors

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.420
that had taken decades to become large enough to detect.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.220
Engineers and public health officials

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.900
in the late 19th and early 20th centuries

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.720
faced frequent catastrophic fires in factories,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.200
theaters, schools and steamships.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.540
Asbestos, already known for its heat resistance

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.420
since Roman times, offered a practical solution

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.800
once large-scale mining began in Quebec

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.920
and South Africa in the 1870s.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:34.320
Companies such as John's Manville and Turner and Newall

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.040
promoted the mineral as a way to protect property

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:38.100
and save lives.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.700
Municipal codes soon required asbestos insulation

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.300
in new public buildings because the same fibers

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.340
that refused to burn also refused to conduct heat.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.860
Promising lower fuel costs for factories and homes.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.580
At the time, the only documented hazard was the dust

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.960
that irritated workers' eyes and throats during installation.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.100
That problem was addressed by better ventilation

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:03.680
rather than by questioning the material itself

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.540
since the visible irritation appeared immediately

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.000
while any deeper effects remained invisible for years.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.760
Between 1900 and 1970, asbestos was installed

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.980
in millions of structures across North America and Europe.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:22.140
The US Navy specified it for nearly every vessel built

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.100
during World War II because the mineral could be sprayed

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.700
or wrapped around pipes and bulkheads in a single pass.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.280
Post-war suburban schools used it in ceiling tiles

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.620
and pipe lagging because the same durability

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.940
that survived naval combat also promised decades

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.480
of fire protection with almost no maintenance.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.860
Manufacturers published brochures calling it

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.240
the Miracle Mineral and cited its record

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.320
of preventing boiler explosions and electrical fires.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.780
Early medical papers, including a 1907 British report

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:57.540
on asbestos factory workers and a 1924 study

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.760
by Dr. W. E. Cook that named asbestosis,

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.680
described lung scarring.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.200
Those papers were read mainly as occupational hygiene issues

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.340
that could be solved on the factory floor

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.580
rather than as signals that the material

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:14.640
might remain dangerous long after installation crews

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:15.900
had finished their work.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:21.020
By the 1950s, annual global production exceeded two million tons

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:25.680
with the United States alone importing roughly 750,000 tons

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:26.760
in peak years.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:30.540
The fiber's chemical stability, the very property

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.780
that made asbestos fireproof also kept them intact

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.500
inside lung tissue for 20 to 50 years.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.140
Once inhaled, the microscopic needles triggered chronic inflammation

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.360
that eventually produced asbestosis

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.200
and decades later, mesothelioma and lung cancers.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.860
Because the diseases appeared long after installation crews

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:53.460
had moved on, builders and regulators

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:55.920
saw no immediate link between the material

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.360
and the rising death toll.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.060
A 1962 nature paper by Christopher Wagner and colleagues

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:05.760
in South Africa finally tied closer to light asbestos

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.420
to mesothelioma clusters.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.920
Yet widespread use continued for another decade

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.960
because the connection still looked like a rare occupational

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:16.940
problem rather than a broad public health risk.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.860
Second order effects compounded the harm.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.520
Firefighters who entered burning buildings

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.300
wearing protective gear shed fibers into the air they breathed

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.380
when the gear itself began to degrade.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.760
School maintenance workers disturbed ceiling tiles

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.440
during routine repairs and released new clouds

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.560
of the same fibers.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.680
Family members developed disease from fibers carried home

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.840
on workers clothing.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.860
Estimates from the World Health Organization

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.880
and national registries suggest that asbestos

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:50.960
related diseases still claim roughly 40,000 lives worldwide

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:51.780
each year.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.800
Most of them in countries that banned the mineral

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.980
only after the 1980s.

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.240
Law suits against manufacturers began in earnest

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:01.700
in the 1970s.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.480
Those suits led to the bankruptcy of Johns Manville in 1982

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.020
and the creation of compensation trusts

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:12.060
that have paid out more than 30 billion dollars.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:15.620
Most Western nations banned or severely restricted

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:19.020
asbestos between 1989 and 2005.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.020
Russia, China and India continued mining and use

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.620
into the 21st century.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.360
Replacement materials such as fiberglass

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.240
and cellulose insulation carry their own installation hazards

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.960
and the cost of removing asbestos from existing buildings

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:37.620
has sometimes delayed other safety upgrades.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.340
Today, the latency period means new cases continue to appear

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:45.160
among people exposed in the 1970s.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.300
Legacy installations in older housing stocks

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.740
still require careful management rather than simple removal.

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:55.260
When the defining virtue of a material or technology

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.460
is extreme durability, planners must ask

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.220
where those durable particles will eventually lodge

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:03.220
if they escape their intended setting.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:06.660
Long latency periods between exposure

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:09.420
and detectable harm can defeat the feedback loops

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.500
that normally prompt course corrections.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:15.120
Anyone evaluating a new substance or system

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:18.600
should therefore treat persistence itself as a risk factor

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.160
and insist on early independent tracking

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:23.480
of biological or environmental fate

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.980
rather than waiting for obvious body counts.

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.060
The same logic now applies to the long-term behavior

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.920
of certain modern polymers and chemical additives

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:34.960
that resist breakdown in the environment.

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.380
What early tracking systems would you put in place

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:39.900
before a widespread adoption?

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:42.600
And that's where today's story leaves us.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:45.500
If it changed how you see one everyday decision,

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:46.700
that's the whole point.

